  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 PM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonably cool this weekend through the middle of next \nweek.\n\nAn upper low remains centered over the eastern Great Lakes this \nafternoon. Surface high pressure over central US is slowly pushing \ntowards the area over the next few days. Cold air advection behind \nyesterday's cold front has pushed temperatures back to near normal \nwith temperatures currently in the lower 60s. High temperatures \nSunday will be similar in the lower to mid 60s. There will be a \nslight warm up Mon thru Wed as high pressure centers over the area \nwith highs in the upper 60s to near 70F. Dry weather continues with \nmostly sunny skies through at least mid week.\n\nLow temperatures late tonight/early Sunday morning will be in the \nmid to upper 30s inland and lower/mid 40s along the coast. Patchy \nfrost is possible, particularly in the Piedmont and counties that \nhave already ended their growing season, but there could be some \nfrost into interior portions of S-central VA and NE NC as well. \nGiven dewpoints expected in the mid 30s, light NW winds, and some \ncloud cover over the southern half of the area overnight, have \nraised low temperatures up to 5 degrees across the area. Based on \nmodel ensembles and hi-res guidance, the NBM low temperatures are an \noutlier. With this, no frost advisories have been issued for \ncounties where the growing season continues.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The weather pattern potentially becomes more \namplified later next week with low-end rain chances returning \nfollowed by a brief surge of colder air.\n\nThe 12z/07 EPS, GEFS, and GEPS all continue to depict an anomalous \n500mb trough developing across the southeast and Mid-Atlantic by \nThurs-Fri of next week. Low-end rain chances return later \nThursday/Thursday night with the potential for developing low \npressure ahead of the deepening trough. Confidence in details at \nthis forecast range is low, but the GEFS and EPS show a 40-80% \nchance of at least 0.01\" of rain with the highest chances in the N \nand a 10-20% chance of at least 0.5\" of rain. A surge of colder air \nfollows with below average temperatures favored by Friday-Saturday \nof next week. With this trend, there is a chance this could end \nthe growing season for the remaining areas.",
